---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/art-history/expert/art-historiography-and-method">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the methodological summit of Art History: it assumes you have already walked the
    survey. Come to it from "Renaissance and Baroque" (intermediate) — Vasari's century is the
    discipline's raw material — "Art beyond the West" (intermediate), whose content the global
    turn reorganises, and the advanced pair "Modern and contemporary art" and "Art market and
    museums", which supply the contemporary field and the institutions this lesson audits.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>trace the historiographic arc from Vasari's <em>Lives</em> through the German method wars to today's pluralism;</li>
    <li>name where each major method locates art's meaning — the hand, the form, the period's intention, the symbol, the cultural memory — and what each cannot see;</li>
    <li>apply Panofsky's three ascending levels to an unfamiliar image;</li>
    <li>read purpose, audience and the global turn as historiographic questions, not just topics;</li>
    <li>run the standing question set — who wrote this account, with what tools, for whom — on any account of any artwork.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Historiography of art is the <strong>history lesson's "history of history" applied to art</strong>:
    every generation of art historians wrote with assumptions their successors exposed — and the
    discipline's self-corrections (feminist, postcolonial, visual-culture) are among the
    humanities' clearest examples of method reforming itself. The syllabus pairs this lesson
    with the <em>global contemporary</em> content area (materials and techniques, purpose and
    audience, interactions across cultures, theories and interpretations), and the pairing is
    methodologically exact: the contemporary field is where every historiographic question —
    whose canon, whose tools, whose categories — is being answered in real time, under the
    discipline's eyes. To study method is to study how we know what we claim about art; the
    contemporary field is the laboratory bench where those claims are currently being made.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="Vasari, the German methods, the critical turns, the expanded field" widget="equationStep" config={histCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four steps are the discipline's biography, and each
    step corrects the one before it. Step 1 shows the founding assumptions being installed
    (genius, progress, the classical standard); step 2 shows them being replaced by instruments
    — Wölfflin's categories, Riegl's Kunstwollen, Panofsky's ladder; step 3 turns the audit on
    the canon itself; step 4 widens the objects until the discipline includes interfaces and
    neuroaesthetics. Move through the steps and watch one reflex repeat: every generation asks
    where meaning lives, and every answer exposes the previous answer's blind spots.
  </p>

  <h2>The historiographic arc: how the discipline made itself</h2>
  <p>
    The story begins with Giorgio Vasari, whose <em>Lives of the Artists</em> (1550) effectively
    invented the genre: biography-driven, teleological (art progressing toward the perfection of
    Michelangelo), Tuscan-proud — and so influential that its assumptions (genius, progress,
    the classical standard) structured the discipline for four centuries. Johann Joachim
    Winckelmann's <em>History of Ancient Art</em> (1764) added the period-style concept and set
    Greek art as the universal pinnacle: art history as the story of styles rising and declining
    against a fixed ideal. The <strong>German method wars</strong> of 1890–1940 then turned the
    story inside out. Heinrich Wölfflin (<em>Principles of Art History</em>, 1915) built
    <strong>formal analysis</strong>: pairs of categories (linear/painterly, plane/recession,
    closed/open form) applied to any period — style as a grammar readable without biography.
    Alois Riegl proposed the <em>Kunstwollen</em> — each era has its own artistic will, so
    "decline" is a category error — and extended the field's sympathy to late antiquity and
    ornament. Aby Warburg traced images' <em>afterlife</em> (survival and return of classical
    pathos formulas across a millennium) and founded the library as laboratory. Erwin Panofsky
    systematised <strong>iconography</strong> into three ascending levels — pre-iconographic
    description (a man raises his hat), iconographic analysis (a gentleman greets, per social
    convention), iconological interpretation (what the gesture reveals about the period's
    worldview) — giving the discipline its most used analytic ladder. Each method was a
    hypothesis about where art's meaning lives: in the hand (connoisseurship), the form
    (Wölfflin), the period's intention (Riegl), the symbol (Panofsky), the cultural memory
    (Warburg) — and the discipline's modern pluralism is the accumulated set.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="rebuild the arc — how the discipline made itself" widget="order" config={ahmArcOrder} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "art history is a fixed chronology of styles and masters."
    The chronology is a <em>product</em> of particular methods with particular assumptions —
    Vasari's progress narrative served Tuscan court politics; Winckelmann's Greek pinnacle
    served Enlightenment aesthetics; both were later exposed as choices. The historiographic
    reflex — asking who wrote this account, with what tools, for what audience — is what
    distinguishes the expert from the tourist, and it applies as sharply to today's survey
    textbooks as to Vasari.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the faulty line — the tourist's summary" widget="spot-error" config={ahmChronSpot} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> you have the arc — how the discipline built and rebuilt its
    instruments from Vasari to Panofsky. The next four sections run those instruments over the
    syllabus's four contemporary strands, starting with the one that breaks the old evidence
    base first: materials that refuse to be stable objects.
  </p>

  <h2>Materials, processes and techniques in global contemporary art</h2>
  <p>
    The first contemporary strand sounds like connoisseurship's territory and is actually its
    revolution: contemporary art's materials <em>are</em> its method problem. The media
    inventory — video and film installation, performance and its documentation, net art,
    software-based and generative work, institutional interventions, ephemeral social
    practice, and now blockchain tokens — breaks the discipline's traditional evidentiary base,
    because there is often no unique, stable object to analyse. The methodological responses
    define the field: <strong>technical art history</strong> (the laboratory methods —
    X-radiography, infrared reflectography, pigment analysis, MA-XRF scanning — that the
    attribution chapter details, now applied to questions of process and making: how a
    contemporary painter builds a surface, or whether a "factory-made" readymade hides the
    artist's hand), <strong>conservation of the ephemeral</strong> (Nam June Paik's video walls
    and deteriorating tape; performance preserved in scores, instructions and re-enactment —
    Marina Abramović's 2010 <em>The Artist Is Present</em> restaged by performers under her
    protocol), and <strong>process documentation as primary source</strong> (the studio
    archive, the install record, the code repository). The historiographic point: as the
    media changed, what counts as "the work" was renegotiated — a conceptual artwork may
    legally and philosophically be the certificate, the instruction sheet, or the database
    entry, and method must follow the object rather than force the object into the old
    methods.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="file the responses — method follows the object" widget="sort-buckets" config={ahmMediaBuckets} />

  <h2>Purpose and audience in global contemporary art</h2>
  <p>
    The second strand asks the oldest questions about the newest art: <strong>what is it for,
    and who is it for?</strong> The historical answers structure the inquiry — medieval
    devotional function, Renaissance patronage (Lorenzo's commissions, the Church's altars),
    the academies' state purposes, the nineteenth century's dealer-critic system (Durand-Ruel
    and the Impressionists: the market becoming patron), the museum's civic-educational
    mission — and the contemporary field multiplies the answers: the biennale (global
    exhibition circuit: Venice from 1895, now over 300 biennials worldwide), the art fair
    (Basel and its satellites: the market's calendar), the museum blockbuster, the corporate
    collection, the Instagram feed (audience without institution), and art commissioned for
    social contexts (public sculpture, community practice). The theoretical instruments:
    Pierre Bourdieu's <em>Distinction</em> (1979) read taste as class positioning and the
    "field of cultural production" as an economy of symbolic capital — which turns museum
    attendance demographics and fair price structures into scholarly <em>data</em> rather than
    gossip; and the audience studies tradition (visitor research, the "reception" strand of
    Iser and Jauss adapted to visual art) that measures what the purpose-theories assume.
    The worked case: when museums publish visitor demographics, they are doing Bourdieu's
    sociology on themselves — and the persistent gaps (class, ethnicity, education) are the
    empirical trace of the audience question the discipline keeps asking.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="pair each era with its answer to 'who is the art for?'" widget="match" config={ahmPurposeMatch} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "the artist's purpose settles the work's meaning." Purpose
    is plural and positional: a work made as critique is acquired as investment, exhibited as
    education, and reposted as decoration — four purposes, none false. Methodologically, the
    historian asks what the work did <em>in each context</em> rather than which context is
    real; the "original intention" is one document in the file, not the file's conclusion.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="drag the divider — one work, its contexts" widget="compare" config={ahmPurposeCompare} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> the contemporary field has forced method to follow the object —
    ephemeral media renegotiated what counts as "the work", and audience research turned
    purpose into measurable data. The next strand is the one where historiography and the
    contemporary field visibly converge: the geography of the story itself.
  </p>

  <h2>Interactions within and across cultures: the global turn</h2>
  <p>
    The third strand is where historiography and the contemporary field most visibly
    converge, because "global contemporary" is itself a historiographic claim. The traditional
    method had a geography: Western Europe (later plus America) as the story, everywhere else
    as exotic context — the "primitivism" frame that read non-Western objects as raw material
    for Western innovation (Picasso and African masks: the masks as catalyst, never as
    makers' art history). The corrections came in waves. The postcolonial critique (Said's
    <em>Orientalism</em> 1978 reframed how the West depicted its others) reached art history
    as method: asking not just what is depicted but who looked, who collected, and what power
    the collection enacted — the provenance research that now drives restitution claims is
    this critique institutionalised. The landmark exhibition <em>Magiciens de la Terre</em>
    (Paris, 1989) staged a global map — Aboriginal sand painting beside Richard Long — and
    its controversies (decontextualisation, the "west plus the rest" hang) taught the field
    more than its successes. Since then the structure shifted: biennales across Asia, Africa
    and Latin America; scholarship decentering Europe (the "global" turn in art history —
    James Elkins' and David Summers' attempts at world frameworks; the journal
    <em>World Art</em>); and the contemporary market's actual geography (Chinese
    contemporary art's emergence in the 2000s; African pavilions multiplying at Venice).
    The methodological lesson: interactions are not additive (add chapters about elsewhere to
    the old story) but structural — trade routes, empires, diasporas and media flows become
    the spine, and "the West" becomes one node in a network rather than the network's centre.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="stage the 1989 hang — the global map in a room" widget="branch" config={ahmGlobalBranch} />

  <h2>Theories and interpretations: the method wars continued</h2>
  <p>
    The fourth strand is where the discipline's interpretive apparatus meets its hardest
    contemporary cases, and the historiographic arc supplies the toolkit. Formal analysis
    (Wölfflin) survives as description's discipline — every strong interpretation still
    starts with what is visibly there. Iconography (Panofsky) scales up: the contemporary
    work's symbols (the readymade, the flag, the photograph-within-the-painting) read through
    their cultural codes. The <strong>social history of art</strong> — Antal, Hauser's
    <em>Social History of Art</em> (1951), T.J. Clark's <em>Painting of Modern Life</em>
    (1984) — reads form as sedimented social relation: Manet's Olympia as class and commerce,
    not just brushwork. <strong>Feminist method</strong> arrives with Linda Nochlin's 1971
    essay "Why Have There Been No Great Women Artists?" — the question reframed from talent
    to institutions (academies that barred women from life drawing, patronage networks that
    excluded them): a demonstration that the discipline's categories themselves were the
    finding. Griselda Pollock and Rozsika Parker built the programme; queer theory,
    postcolonial and decolonial readings extended the audit. The contemporary field consumes
    all of it simultaneously — a single installation may be read formally, iconographically,
    sociologically and politically in one catalogue essay — and the expert's skill is
    <strong>methodological self-awareness</strong>: knowing which lens is in use, what it
    reveals, what it structurally cannot see, and saying so. The theory chapter's lenses,
    that is, are not opinions but instruments with documented strengths and blind spots.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="fill the blanks — naming the lens" widget="cloze" config={ahmLensCloze} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> "postmodern pluralism means any interpretation is as good
    as any other." The methods compete and correct each other, which is the opposite of
    anything-goes: an interpretation is accountable to the object (what is visibly there),
    to context (documents, reception history) and to its own stated method. Reading Warhol's
    Brillo boxes as pure mysticism fails the first test; reading them as purely commercial
    fails the third. The discipline's pluralism is a bench of instruments, not an absence of
    standards.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="true or false — pluralism's standards" widget="judge" config={ahmPluralismJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> all four contemporary strands have now been read through the
    historical toolkit — and the toolkit survived by stating its own limits. The last section
    turns from reading to doing: what the expert's practice looks like at research scale.
  </p>

  <h2>Doing art history at the expert level</h2>
  <p>
    The method's practice, at research scale, has a learnable shape. <strong>Every attribution
    carries a method</strong>: connoisseurship (Morelli's ears and fingernails — the habits
    that betray a hand), documentary evidence (contracts, inventories, correspondence),
    technical analysis (the laboratory instruments) — triangulated, never single-sourced: the
    statistics lesson's convergent evidence. <strong>The period eye</strong> (Michael
    Baxandall's <em>Painting and Experience</em>, 1972): reconstructing what fifteenth-century
    Florentines brought to a painting — sermons, dance figures, the commercial skill of
    gauging barrels — so that historical viewing becomes a recoverable skill rather than an
    anachronism. <strong>The argument structure</strong>: a claim about meaning, evidence from
    form, context and documents, objections addressed — the critique lesson's Feldman order
    at research scale. And <strong>writing as craft</strong>: Gombrich (<em>Art and
    Illusion</em>, 1960 — perception as the historian's problem), Clark, Svetlana Alpers —
    the discipline's best prose is itself a method, because an argument about seeing must be
    seen through.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Name Panofsky's three ascending levels in order, using his own hat-raising example as the illustration.</li>
    <li>A catalogue essay reads Manet's <em>Olympia</em> purely as brushwork innovation. Which method is in use, and what does it structurally miss?</li>
    <li>Why is "add chapters about elsewhere to the old story" the wrong model for the global turn — and what is the structural alternative this lesson describes?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Pre-iconographic description (a man raises his hat),
    iconographic analysis (a gentleman greets, per social convention), iconological
    interpretation (what the gesture reveals about the period's worldview) — description,
    then convention, then worldview. (2) Formal analysis in the Wölfflin lineage: it is
    disciplined about what is visibly there, but form alone cannot register <em>Olympia</em>
    as class and commerce — the social history of art (Clark) exists precisely for what the
    formal lens misses. (3) Because interactions are not additive: trade routes, empires,
    diasporas and media flows become the spine of the story, and "the West" becomes one node
    in a network rather than the network's centre. Appended chapters leave the old geography
    in charge; the structural correction rebuilds the map.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Historiography studies how we know what we claim about art — the chronology is a product of methods with assumptions.</li>
    <li>Vasari installed genius and progress; Winckelmann added period-style and the Greek pinnacle; both were later exposed as choices.</li>
    <li>The German method wars built the instruments: Wölfflin's formal pairs, Riegl's Kunstwollen, Warburg's afterlife, Panofsky's three-level ladder.</li>
    <li>Contemporary media break the stable-object evidence base; method must follow the object.</li>
    <li>Purpose is plural and positional — the historian asks what the work did in each context.</li>
    <li>The global turn is structural, not additive: the West becomes one node in a network.</li>
    <li>Methodological self-awareness — naming the lens in use, its strengths and blind spots — is the expert's core skill.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling expert lesson "Attribution, conservation and forgery" takes one strand of this
    chapter — technical art history and triangulated evidence — and develops it into a full
    method: connoisseurship, laboratory instruments, documentary evidence and the forger's
    counter-move. Read the two together and the standing question set gains its hardest test
    cases: works that lie about their own making.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>The global-contemporary debate.</strong> Whether "global contemporary art" is a
    period, a market category, or a historiographic correction is live scholarship: Terry
    Smith's <em>Contemporary Art: World Currents</em> maps the currents; Hans Belting's
    <em>Globally</em> projects interrogate whether the "contemporary" is Western modernism's
    epilogue or a genuinely plural condition; the companion critique (from decolonial
    scholars) asks whether global circuits reproduce the asymmetries they claim to dissolve.
    The underlying data problem — languages, archives, access — makes this partly an
    infrastructure question, and digital humanities tools (linked open data for provenance,
    multilingual corpora) are the field's current build-out.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The expanded field.</strong> Visual culture studies (advertising, games,
    interfaces as legitimate objects), neuroaesthetics (Zeki's neurobiology of viewing —
    contested for its reduction, informative for its methods), environmental humanities
    (climate as art-historical subject), and the return of the technical (conservation
    science feeding back into interpretation) all widen what the discipline reads. The
    historiographic irony to hold: the field founded on Vasari's biographies now includes
    x-ray fluorescence spectra and Instagram metrics — and the method wars of the 1890s–1970s
    were the argument that got it there. The discipline is its own best case study.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The permanent reflex.</strong> What the expert carries out of this chapter is not
    a list of methods but a standing question set, applicable to any account of any art:
    Who produced this account, and in what institutional position? What does the method
    assume about where meaning lives? Whose art does the category make visible — and whose
    does it make illegible? What evidence would change this interpretation? The contemporary
    field, restless and globalised, is simply where those questions are loudest; the
    historiographic training is what lets you hear them in a 1550 page or a 2026 catalogue
    essay alike.
  </p>

